# Coco Tree Service — Project Rules

## Project Goal

Build a premium, high-performance landing page for Coco Tree Service focused on trust, professionalism, safety, speed, and conversion.

The visual direction is natural and cinematic, using forests, trees, real photography, professional equipment, workers, trucks, chippers, chainsaws, and Bobcats as visual elements.

The website must communicate that professional tree work and respect for the natural environment can coexist.

The primary conversion target of the website is driving users to request a free estimate ("Get a Free Estimate").

## Core Principles

- Prioritize clean, maintainable, production-ready code.
- Prefer simple solutions over unnecessary complexity.
- Do not install dependencies unless there is a clear technical reason.
- Reuse existing components before creating new ones.
- Do not duplicate code.
- Do not create abstractions without a real need.
- Keep components focused on a single responsibility.
- Do not modify unrelated files when implementing a feature.
- Preserve existing functionality when modifying components.
- Do not rewrite working code unnecessarily.

## Technology

Use the project's existing technology stack:

- Next.js (App Router)
- React
- TypeScript
- Tailwind CSS
- GSAP
- ScrollTrigger
- Lenis
- Lucide React
- Zod when validation is required

Do not introduce another framework, animation library, UI library, or state-management library without first establishing a concrete technical need.

## Server and Client Components (RSC Strategy)

- Keep pp/page.tsx as a **React Server Component (RSC)**. Do NOT place "use client" at the top of page.tsx or global layout files.
- Compose the main landing page by importing individual section wrappers (e.g., <HeroSection />, <ServicesSection />, <EstimateModal />).
- Declare "use client" strictly inside lower-level component files or section containers that require interactive behavior, DOM listeners, React hooks, GSAP timelines, ScrollTrigger, or Lenis instances.

## TypeScript

- Use TypeScript throughout the project.
- Avoid ny unless there is a documented technical reason.
- Prefer explicit interfaces or types for reusable component data (centralized in 	ypes/).
- Keep component props strictly typed.
- Resolve TypeScript errors instead of suppressing them.

## Components & Architecture

- Build reusable components when the same UI pattern appears more than once.
- Keep large sections divided into logical components.
- Separate UI primitives (components/ui/), layout containers (components/layout/), interactive forms (components/forms/), and page sections (components/sections/).
- Primary conversion flows, including the estimate form (EstimateForm) and modal triggers, belong strictly inside components/forms/ or components/ui/.
- Keep content/data separate from presentation components by putting static models inside data/.

## Styling

- Use Tailwind CSS for component styling.
- Follow the project's Design System.
- Avoid arbitrary values when an existing design token or utility can be used.
- Do not introduce a second styling system.
- Maintain consistent spacing, typography, borders, radii, and visual hierarchy.

## Responsive Design

Every visual component must be considered for:

- Desktop
- Tablet
- Mobile

Do not treat mobile as an afterthought.

Animations must degrade gracefully on smaller devices.

## Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML.
- Buttons must be actual buttons.
- Links must be actual links.
- Images require meaningful alt text when appropriate.
- Interactive elements must remain usable with keyboard navigation.
- Maintain sufficient visual contrast.
- Respect prefers-reduced-motion for non-essential animations.

## Performance

- Optimize images and video.
- Use lazy loading when appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary client-side JavaScript.
- Prefer Server Components unless client-side behavior is required.
- Do not load heavy libraries or assets unnecessarily.
- Keep animations efficient.

## Animation

GSAP and ScrollTrigger are the primary tools for complex motion.

Lenis is used for smooth scrolling where appropriate.

Use CSS for simple hover states and micro-interactions when GSAP is unnecessary.

Animations must support the design and conversion goals rather than exist purely as decoration.

## Content

Do not invent factual business information such as:

- prices
- addresses
- phone numbers
- certifications
- years of experience
- service areas
- testimonials
- guarantees

Use placeholders or clearly marked temporary content until verified information is provided.

## Development Workflow

Before implementing a new feature:

1. Inspect the existing project structure.
2. Check whether a reusable component already exists.
3. Check whether an existing dependency can solve the problem.
4. Implement only the required change.
5. Test the result.
6. Check TypeScript and linting.
7. Check responsive behavior.
8. Review the final code for unnecessary duplication or complexity.

## Git

Keep commits focused and meaningful.

Do not mix unrelated changes in the same commit.

Do not commit secrets, credentials, API keys, or .env.local.

## Agent Behavior

Before making significant architectural changes, explain the reason.

If requirements are ambiguous and the decision could materially affect architecture, ask before proceeding.

Do not make broad changes when a targeted change is sufficient.
